{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CgHhVF1DC7qwfuRZ89ACE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01A9C6i3PyBdE4YS9f78pR5c","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"fix_prompt":"# Fix Prompt for Scenario INT-900: MCP /mcp endpoint responds 200 to a valid initialize POST\n\n## Suite resolution (do not re-derive)\n\n- Subject `service:` is `mcp-service`.\n- `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml` declares that service with\n  `path: services/mcp-service`, and its testing commands run\n  `./tests/mcp/...`. `CLAUDE.md` states the binding contract explicitly:\n  *\"Which suite owns it comes from `docs/architecture/architecture.yaml`:\n  the tests/<service>/ tree whose `service:` matches the scenario's. Here\n  that is `mcp`, rooted at `tests/mcp`, so definitions belong in\n  `tests/mcp/steps/`.\"*\n- **Suite root:** `tests/mcp`. **Only writable tree:** `tests/mcp/steps/`.\n\n## State of the suite right now\n\n`tests/mcp/steps/` **does not exist**. There are no sibling definitions,\nno shared state type, no helpers, and no `Register` function anywhere in\nthe module — `tests/mcp/mcp_test.go` is the only `.go` file in the repo.\nSo there is nothing to reuse and nothing to duplicate: this fix\n*establishes* the suite's step vocabulary, its state type and its single\n`Register` call site in one pass.\n\nTwo consequences, both deliberate:\n\n1. The package must be self-contained. `tests/mcp/mcp_test.go` imports\n   `fixture/buildtests/tests/mcp/scenarios`, which does not exist and\n   which this fix may **not** create (it is outside `tests/mcp/steps/`).\n   The `steps` package therefore defines its own minimal registry\n   (`Suite` + `Step` + `Resolve`/`Run`) so it compiles on its own. Do not\n   import `.../tests/mcp/scenarios` — that would not build.\n2. Both new files carry `//go:build bdd`, matching the only existing file\n   in the suite (`mcp_test.go`). Keep the tag consistent with the suite;\n   if the suite ever drops it, drop it here too.\n\n## Neighbour sweep — performed, came back empty\n\n`docs/scenarios.yaml` contains exactly **one** scenario, `INT-900`. There\nare no scenarios of the same `service:` following it, so there are no\nneighbour steps to collect. Nothing is being left for a later cycle.\n\nBecause there are no neighbours to generalise *from*, all three patterns\nbelow are parameterised instead of copied literally, so the next\n`mcp-service` scenario the registry gains binds for free:\n\n| Step text (INT-900) | Pattern | Capture(s) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| the MCP server is running on its configured port | `^the (.+) server is running on its configured port$` | server name |\n| the Claude User posts a valid JSON-RPC initialize request to /mcp | `^the (.+) posts a valid JSON-RPC ([\\w./-]+) request to (/\\S*)$` | actor, JSON-RPC method, endpoint path |\n| the server returns HTTP 200 | `^the server returns HTTP (\\d{3})$` | status code |\n\nAll three are anchored `^…$` and mutually disjoint — no step text can\nmatch two of them, so no step resolves ambiguously.\n\n## Required Changes\n\n### Change #1: create the suite's local harness and shared state type\n\n**File:** `tests/mcp/steps/suite.go`\n**Action:** Use the **Write** tool (new file).\n**Content:**\n\n```go\n//go:build bdd\n\n// Local harness for the mcp suite's step definitions.\n//\n// The suite owns its registry because the step definitions must compile\n// on their own: `World` is the state every definition in this package\n// shares, and `Suite.Step` is the single registration form the step\n// vocabulary is written in.\npackage steps\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n)\n\n// World is the state every step definition in this suite shares.\n// A Given fills it in, a When records what happened, a Then reads it.\ntype World struct {\n\t// BaseURL is the root the suite posts to, e.g. \"http://127.0.0.1:8080\".\n\tBaseURL string\n\t// Port is the configured port the Given resolved and reached.\n\tPort int\n\n\t// Requested reports whether any request has been sent yet, so a Then\n\t// can tell \"no request was made\" apart from \"the status was zero\".\n\tRequested bool\n\t// LastPath is the endpoint the most recent request went to.\n\tLastPath string\n\t// LastStatus is the HTTP status code of the most recent response.\n\tLastStatus int\n\t// LastBody is the body of the most recent response, verbatim.\n\tLastBody string\n}\n\n// StepFunc runs one step. args holds the capture groups of the pattern\n// that matched, in order.\ntype StepFunc func(w *World, args []string) error\n\ntype definition struct {\n\tpattern *regexp.Regexp\n\tfn      StepFunc\n}\n\n// Suite is this suite's step registry.\ntype Suite struct {\n\tdefs []definition\n}\n\n// NewSuite returns a suite with every definition in this package bound.\nfunc NewSuite() *Suite {\n\ts := &Suite{}\n\tRegister(s)\n\treturn s\n}\n\n// Step registers fn under an anchored regexp.\nfunc (s *Suite) Step(pattern string, fn StepFunc) {\n\ts.defs = append(s.defs, definition{\n\t\tpattern: regexp.MustCompile(pattern),\n\t\tfn:      fn,\n\t})\n}\n\n// Resolve returns the one definition matching text. A step that matches\n// none, or more than one, is a registry error rather than a test\n// failure, and says so.\nfunc (s *Suite) Resolve(text string) (StepFunc, []string, error) {\n\tvar (\n\t\tfound StepFunc\n\t\targs  []string\n\t\thits  []string\n\t)\n\tfor _, d := range s.defs {\n\t\tm := d.pattern.FindStringSubmatch(text)\n\t\tif m == nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\thits = append(hits, d.pattern.String())\n\t\tfound, args = d.fn, m[1:]\n\t}\n\tswitch len(hits) {\n\tcase 0:\n\t\treturn nil, nil, fmt.Errorf(\"step is unbound: no definition in the mcp suite matches %q\", text)\n\tcase 1:\n\t\treturn found, args, nil\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn nil, nil, fmt.Errorf(\"step %q is ambiguous: %d definitions match it (%v); exactly one must\", text, len(hits), hits)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Run resolves text and executes it aga